Chili's government is coming under  extreme pressure as violent street  protests show no sign of abating  president sebastián piñera has promised  of a wage hike and other reforms has  done nothing to appease the  demonstrators who accused the police of  using excessive force the protests  started last month sparked by a hike in  subway fares they've since widened into  a mass movement against inequality  demonstrators clash with police in  Santiago and appeased by the  government's latest concessions  protesters build barricades and police  respond but according to Chilean  prosecutors police tactics do not stop  with tear gas water cannon and arrests  they are investigating more than 800  allegations of abuse by police including  torture rape and beatings chelates  president says his government has  nothing to hide  he's vowed to investigate crimes  committed by police with the same  tenacity as they pursued demonstrators  who destroy property with the same force  and rigor any appearance that has been  committed any lack of respect or  excessive use of force will also be  investigated by the prosecution and the  courts of justice that's what happens in  a democracy with the rule of law what  started as a protest over a rise in  subway fares almost three weeks ago has  grown into a movement against what many  on the streets see as the yawning gap  between rich and poor and the lack of  support for ordinary people highways are  just one of the many services Chileans  pay for directly truck drivers blocked  the Ring roads around the capital they  say tolls for the roads operated by  private companies are too high  if these highway agreements have been in  place since 2006 the companies have  earned back the investment eight times  over and they keep charging us and  people aren't dead some demonstrators  want a new constitution for Chile one  that protects social services and  resources from being privatized for them  increasing the minimum wage is just not  enough  GWS Nicole clearly is covering the story  for us in Santiago she says mistrust of  the government and police remains high  while president sebastián piñera is  trying to appease protesters with  symbolic gestures like the approval of  minimum wage subsidies many see right  through him in recent days he has  embarked on a charm offensive of sorts  giving lots of interviews visiting  victims of police brutality in the  hospital but too many people out in the  streets protesting again today it's too  little too late and most importantly it  comes from a president that they want  gone the protests actually spread out  today they didn't only take place in the  centric Plaza d'Italia Square people  also gathered in Santiago's financial  district and what struck me personally  the most was how far riot police was  driving protestors into residential  areas  there were hordes of people that had  nothing to do with the protests hiding  out info yeas not daring to go out in  the street  fearing the lingering tear gas or being  hit by stray rubber bullets  so there's a deep feeling of insecurity  and a huge mistrust in the police force  and that is something the president will  have to tackle if he wants to keep these  protests from escalating
 

